Hello 3Dprinting.com team,

a targeted suggestion for your software overview rather than a general pitch.

Solidon3D sits in a category that is hard to place: it is not a CAD system and
not a mesh editor. It takes a downloaded model and lets you edit it at feature
level without CAD knowledge. Click a hole and its dimensions appear on the
model itself, type 8 instead of 6 and the countersink follows, pick a fit for
the screw, read the check report before you export, hand the file to the
slicer you already use.

With version 0.5.0 one line in any comparison table changes: the result is no
longer a mesh. Holes, countersinks and threads stay exact through moving,
duplicating and removing, and STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly, so a
part edited in Solidon can be carried into a CAD system.

That is also the reason the category exists at all. CAD packages accept an STL
but cannot grip triangles. Mesh tools edit triangles but never return exact
surfaces, and the two that came closest are discontinued, Meshmixer and
Microsoft's 3D Builder. Parametric customisers only work when the original
author anticipated the parameter.

Other entries that may be relevant for a guide. Licensing is a one-time
purchase in three tiers with no subscription; after a single activation the
application runs fully offline, with no account, no telemetry and no server
requirement, and a machine without a network connection can be activated via a
file and a second device. Platforms are Windows, macOS and Linux, with native
Linux packages. Interface and complete manual are available in six languages.
AI is present, optional, and able to run locally on the user's own GPU, with
no feature depending on it except the chat itself. On the printing side there
are printer profiles including resin devices with pixel size and minimum wall,
printability checks that name the cause rather than just the symptom,
orientation search, plate layout and handover to the common slicers. And
clearances are entered as a reference to the material rather than as a number,
with profiles you can calibrate to your own printer from printed test pieces.

The demo is free and complete, so anything above can be verified directly. If
a screenshot would help to place the entry, I will provide one.

